6778.sol:
Equation of circle $C_1$ be $x^2+y^2=16$
Equation of circle $C_2$ be $(x-\alpha)^2+(y-\beta)^2=25\Rightarrow x^2-2 \alpha x+\alpha^2+y^2-2 \beta y+\beta^2=25$<br/>Equation of common chord is $C_2-C_1=0\Rightarrow-2 \alpha x-2 \beta y+\alpha^2+\beta^2=9<br/>Slope of $common chord =$\frac{-\alpha}{\beta}=\frac{3}{4}
Let $\alpha=-3 \lambda$ and $\beta=4 \lambda$
Maximum length of common chord is diameter of smaller circle $C_1$. So, chord passes through centre of $C_2(0,0)$.
$\begin{aligned}
& \therefore \alpha^2+\beta^2=9 \Rightarrow 9 \lambda^2+16 \lambda^2=9 \Rightarrow \lambda=$\frac{3}{5}$ \\
& \Rightarrow \alpha+\beta=$\frac{-9}{5}+\frac{12}{5}=\frac{3}{5}$
\end{aligned}

